.\" Checked against borealis/borealis.nim and Aureole's production handlers.
.TH BOREALIS 1 "November 22, 2026" CaelWM "CaelWM Manual"
.SH NAME
borealis \- command-line X11 client for Aureole
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B borealis \-h
.br
.B borealis \-v
.br
.B borealis
.I domain action
.RI [ arguments ...]
.br
.B borealis
.I action-command
.RB [ . " action-command ..."]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B borealis
queries and controls
.BR aureole (1)
using its X11 command protocol. Window management is performed by the WM, not
the client. Borealis connects to the display selected by XCB's environment; it
does not use Aetherd's Unix socket or cache and does not require Aetherd for
its own commands.
.PP
.BR aether (1)
is the higher-level controller for layout, application rules, state history
and group workflows. Its command grammar differs from Borealis.
.SH OPTIONS AND ARGUMENTS
.TP 7
.B \-h
As the first argument, print short usage to standard error and exit 0 without
connecting to X11.
.TP
.B \-v
As the first argument, print compiled version and license information to
standard error and exit 0 without connecting to X11. The source version
default is 0.5.2.
.PP
No other global options, long help/version aliases or end-of-options marker
are implemented. Command words and option flags are case-sensitive. Direction
and configuration-choice values are case-insensitive unless stated otherwise.
.PP
WINID is a nonzero unsigned 32-bit hexadecimal X window ID, optionally
prefixed with 0x. Prefer the output form 0x followed by eight lowercase
hexadecimal digits. Where WINID is optional, omission targets the focused
managed window. Collection commands use selectors instead of WINID. window
group requires an explicit ID; window classname always queries focus.
.PP
Signed coordinates and deltas accept decimal integers from -2147483648 through
2147483647. Aureole narrows ordinary move coordinates and resize deltas to
signed 16-bit values; use -32768 through 32767 to avoid wrapping. Bulk move
coordinates also narrow to signed 16-bit values. Group numbers and quit
statuses are unsigned decimal 32-bit
values. Positive signs do not imply relative navigation. The geometry and
stack query parsers retain permissive legacy integer conversion; use canonical
IDs rather than relying on malformed-input acceptance.
.SH WINDOW QUERIES
.TP
.B window focused
Print the focused managed WINID; fail when none exists.
.TP
.B window classname
Print the focused window's WM_CLASS class component. Unavailable or malformed
class data produces empty output; lack of a focused managed window is an
error.
.TP
.B window ids [SELECTOR] [\-\-group GROUP]
Print matching IDs, one per line, in ascending numeric-XID order. Defaults to
mapped managed clients. The group filter may be inserted among the other query
arguments, is allowed once, and must be 1 through group count minus 1. It
narrows the current visibility/selector scope without implying \-\-all.
.TP
.B window count [SELECTOR]
Print a decimal count using the same visibility and selector rules. Does not
accept \-\-group.
.PP
SELECTOR has exactly these supported forms:
.PP
.nf
(no arguments)
CLASS
\-\-all
\-\-all CLASS
CLASS \-\-all
\-\-name PATTERN
\-\-all \-\-name PATTERN
.fi
.PP
CLASS matches the WM_CLASS class component exactly and case-sensitively.
PATTERN is a case-insensitive POSIX extended regular expression, matched
against _NET_WM_NAME, falling back to WM_NAME. Quote patterns in the shell. A
class and title selector cannot be combined. For a title query with \-\-all,
the order shown above is required. Empty matches produce no ID lines or count
0 and succeed; invalid title expressions fail.
.TP
.B window geometry [WINID]
Print logical client geometry as four lines: X=number, Y=number, WIDTH=number
and HEIGHT=number. The values describe the client, not an outer decoration
rectangle.
.TP
.B window group WINID
Print the client's raw decimal WM group ID, including the ungrouped sentinel
where applicable.
.TP
.B window groups \-\-all
Print every managed client's WINID and raw decimal GROUP, separated by a
space, in managed-list order. The flag is mandatory.
.TP
.B window snapshot
Print the normalized snapshot. Current Aureole emits:
.PP
.nf
SNAPSHOT 2
FOCUSED WINID_OR_NONE
CURRENT GROUP
CLIENT WINID GROUP MAPPED TOKEN
\&...
.fi
.PP
MAPPED is 0 or 1; TOKEN is the WM client-identity token. CLIENT records follow
managed-list order. NONE means no focused managed client. Group values are raw
decimal IDs, not EWMH-style rebased indices. Borealis also accepts a SNAPSHOT
1 response from a compatible server.
.TP
.B window stack [WINID]
Print the mapped overlap-connected set containing the reference window, in
physical bottom-to-top stacking order. Connectivity is transitive through
overlapping logical rectangles; this is not simply every window in a group.
.TP
.B window stack-geometries [WINID]
Print the same stack as lines of WINID X Y WIDTH HEIGHT, using decimal
geometry fields, without sorting by ID.
.SH WINDOW ACTIONS
.TP
.B window move [\-\-relative] X Y [WINID]
Set absolute client coordinates, or add signed pixel offsets with
\-\-relative. The flag must precede the numbers. Relative movement can reset
maximized/monocled state before moving.
.TP
.B window resize [\-\-relative] WIDTH HEIGHT [WINID]
Set absolute size or apply signed size deltas. Absolute operands must be
nonnegative signed 32-bit integers; Aureole uses their low 16 bits and
compares the signed narrowed operands against minimum-size hints. Use positive
values at most 32767 for ordinary sizes without signed wraparound; larger
accepted operands may produce surprising minimum-hint results. Relative
resizing applies the WM's resize-hint policy. Resulting geometry can differ
from the requested values.
.TP
.B window maximize [\-\-horizontal|\-\-vertical] [WINID]
Toggle full, horizontal or vertical maximization using the target's monitor
bounds. The axis flag precedes the optional ID. Full maximization uses full
monitor bounds; partial maximization accounts for configured gaps and borders.
.TP
.B window monocle [WINID]
Toggle monocle geometry within the usable monitor region, respecting gaps and
border extent. Repeating the same special mode restores its saved geometry.
.TP
.B window reset [WINID]
Reset special geometry state to the WM's saved original geometry. When special
geometry is reset, Aureole also focuses and raises the target. This is not
Aether's filesystem geometry-history restore.
.TP
.B window close [WINID]
Request graceful WM_DELETE_WINDOW when supported; otherwise the WM kills the X
client connection.
.TP
.B window hide [WINID]
Unmap the target while retaining management. Fail if unknown/unmanaged or
already hidden. A later explicit focus can remap a hidden managed target.
.TP
.B window layer normal|above|overlay [WINID]
Set an explicit persistent stacking layer without changing focus. Only the
lowercase and title-case spellings Normal, Above and Overlay are accepted. The
explicit choice survives hide/remap until the client is unmanaged.
Maximized/monocled normal and above clients use a zoomed tier; overlay remains
above that tier.
.TP
.B window focus WINID
Focus and raise the managed target, mapping it if needed.
.TP
.B window focus \-\-last
Choose the prior eligible mapped window from focus history.
.TP
.B window focus \-\-cardinal DIRECTION
Focus a mapped adjacent candidate chosen by the WM's geometric directional
search. DIRECTION is up/north, down/south, left/west or right/east,
case-insensitively. With no candidate, the request has no effect.
.TP
.B window cycle [\-\-reverse] [\-\-group]
Cycle mapped windows in managed-list order, optionally in reverse or
restricted to the focused window's group. Each flag may occur once, in either
order. This cycles windows, not group IDs.
.TP
.B window stack cycle [WINID]
Focus and raise a window chosen from the reference's overlap-connected stack,
respecting stacking tiers. This is distinct from window cycle. Uses the
focused window as the default reference.
.TP
.B window apply-geometries WINID X Y WIDTH HEIGHT ...
Submit one or more five-argument geometry records as one property-backed
request. Each ID must name a managed client and occur once. X and Y are signed
32-bit decimal; dimensions are 0 through 65535. Aureole validates the full
record set first, then applies move followed by resize per record. Application
is not a rollback transaction, and minimum hints may affect final sizes. It
does not create Aether geometry history. Resetting a maximized, partially
maximized or monocled target before applying the records can focus and raise
that target; the command does not guarantee preservation of focus.
.TP
.B window raise-many WINID ...
Raise one or more distinct mapped managed clients in argument order within
their effective stacking tiers, preserving focus. Last listed is raised last
within its tier. The server rejects duplicates and unmapped/unmanaged targets.
Property-backed bulk requests are limited to 65536 bytes by Aureole.
.SH GROUPS
.TP
.B group count
Print the configured group-slot count (default 10), not the number of active
groups.
.TP
.B group current
Print the focused managed window's raw group ID. With no managed focus, print
4294967295.
.TP
.B group add GROUP [WINID]
Assign the target to a WM group. This changes actual WM membership, unlike
Aether's filesystem group add bookkeeping. An out-of-range destination or
missing target is silently ignored by the WM; this is a one-way action.
.TP
.B group remove [WINID]
Remove WM membership, setting the ungrouped sentinel. Does not close the
client.
.TP
.B group clear GROUP
Remove membership from every client in that group and clear its active state.
Does not close group members. Out-of-range groups are ignored.
.TP
.B group activate GROUP
Map members of a populated inactive group and mark it active. Activation alone
does not focus a member or select a current group. Empty or already active
groups produce no mapping change. Wait for an acknowledgement; invalid group
numbers fail.
.TP
.B group deactivate GROUP
Unmap group members and clear active state. If the focused window belongs to
that group, clear focus and current-group state. Wait for an acknowledgement;
invalid group numbers fail.
.SS Numbering and navigation
The public controller workflow uses group IDs 1 through N minus 1, where N is
group count. IDs pass unchanged through Borealis and Aureole: public group 1
is WM slot 1, not slot 0. Group names such as CODE are Aether policy, not
Borealis arguments.
.PP
The low-level add, clear, activate and deactivate handlers accept slot 0 when
N is positive; their actual WM range is 0 through N minus 1. The IDs query's
\-\-group filter deliberately rejects 0. Neither slot 0 nor an omitted group
is the ungrouped sentinel: that value is 0xffffffff, printed as 4294967295 by
group observations. Multiple groups can be active at once. Current-group
queries reflect focus, not the last activation request.
.PP
There are no Borealis group next, previous, last or focus verbs, and no
relative-group operands. Use Aether's navigation, for example
.nf
aether group focus "$(aether group next)"
.fi
or activate a numeric group and explicitly focus a member. A leading plus on a
number denotes an absolute positive integer, not an offset; negative group
operands are rejected.
.SH WM CONTROL
.TP
.B wm restart
Request Aureole cleanup and exit with status 75. The WM does not relaunch
itself; a supervisor may relaunch it on that status.
.TP
.B wm quit STATUS
Request WM exit with an unsigned 32-bit wire status, interpreted by Aureole as
signed 32-bit. Shell process exit statuses expose only their normal low
exit-status bits. This one-way request does not wait for termination.
.TP
.B wm config KEY VALUE ...
Set a runtime configuration value. There is no configuration query or save
command. Each key requires exactly the argument count shown below. Changes are
one-way requests and are not written to aureolerc.
.SS Configuration values
Boolean keys accept true, yes, t, y or 1 as true, case-insensitively; every
other string becomes false. They are apply_settings, enable_borders,
enable_last_window_focusing, enable_resize_hints, enable_sloppy_focus,
replay_click_on_focus and sticky_windows. apply_settings controls immediate
refresh for subsequent border-setting changes; sticky_windows selects
ungrouped placement for new ordinary clients.
.TP
.B border_style NUMBER
Choose 0 for a full frame, 1 left, 2 top, 3 right, 4 bottom, or 5 corner
segments. Other numbers take the full-frame rendering fallback.
.TP
.B outer_border_width NUMBER
Outer decoration width in pixels.
.TP
.B inner_border_width NUMBER
Inner decoration width in pixels.
.TP
.B outer_color_focused HEX
Focused outer color in hexadecimal ARGB form, for example 0xffff0000.
.TP
.B outer_color_unfocused HEX
Unfocused outer color.
.TP
.B inner_color_focused HEX
Focused inner color.
.TP
.B inner_color_unfocused HEX
Unfocused inner color.
.TP
.B corner_mask NUMBER
Bit mask for corner segments: 8 top left, 4 top right, 2 bottom right and 1
bottom left; add bits to combine corners.
.TP
.B corner_percent NUMBER
Corner segment size relative to the smaller client dimension. Aureole caps the
unsigned value at 100; zero selects full-length segments.
.TP
.B cursor_position POSITION
Pointer-warp position used by geometry actions. POSITION is topleft, topright,
bottomleft, bottomright, middle, center, centre, left, bottom, top, right or
all. Values are case-insensitive.
.TP
.B click_to_focus left|middle|right|none|any
Select the button triggering focus. Values are case-insensitive.
.TP
.B gap_width POSITION NUMBER
Set a monitor-edge gap. Effective POSITION values are top, bottom, left, right
or all. Other cursor-position words pass client parsing but have no
gap-setting effect in Aureole.
.TP
.B groups_nr NUMBER
Set the group-slot count. Shrinking the count activates affected removed
groups and removes their clients' group membership. The implementation accepts
a raw unsigned 32-bit count, including zero; there is no smaller enforced
user-range limit.
.TP
.B pointer_actions LEFT MIDDLE RIGHT
Choose each mouse-button action from nothing, focus, move, resize_corner or
resize_side, case-insensitively.
.TP
.B pointer_modifier alt|super
Select the pointer-action modifier, case-insensitively.
.PP
Configuration key names are case-sensitive. Retained aliases are border_width
for outer_border_width, color_focused for outer_color_focused, color_unfocused
for outer_color_unfocused, internal_border_width for inner_border_width,
internal_color_focused for inner_color_focused and internal_color_unfocused
for inner_color_unfocused.
.PP
Numeric configuration parsing is permissive strtol conversion (base 10, or
base 16 for colors), cast to an unsigned 32-bit wire word; it does not
consistently reject trailing text or negative values. Use complete nonnegative
values for counts, masks, percentages and widths. Parsing success alone is not
confirmation of a valid setting or successful WM execution.
.SH LAYOUTS AND CHAINING
Borealis has move, resize, maximize, monocle, reset, stack queries and bulk
geometry operations. It has no layouts domain or tile, fold, unfold, explode
or unexplode command. Those policy operations belong to Aether.
.PP
A separate period argument chains only the ordinary action dispatcher: wm
config/quit/restart, group add/remove/clear/activate/deactivate and the window
actions move/resize/maximize/monocle/close/hide/reset/layer/cycle/focus.
Queries and the special stack and bulk commands are handled before that
dispatcher and cannot be composed into these chains. Empty segments are
skipped. An acknowledged error stops a chain; one-way actions give no
completion barrier between requests.
.SH OUTPUT AND EXIT STATUS
Queries print the decoded payload without the protocol's OK envelope.
Successful actions normally produce no standard output. Errors and
help/version text go to standard error.
.PP
No arguments prints usage and exits 1. Help and version exit 0. Queries and
acknowledged actions exit 0 on a valid success response and 1 on server
errors, malformed responses, X11 failures or a response timeout of about two
seconds.
.PP
Acknowledged actions are group activate/deactivate and window
hide/reset/layer/stack cycle/apply-geometries/raise-many. Other actions send
one-way ClientMessage requests and normally return 0 after submission. They
can silently have no effect; even an X send error may only print an oops
diagnostic without changing the final status. Exit 0 from such a command does
not establish that Aureole applied it. Some diagnostics retain the
compatibility name windowchef.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.B DISPLAY
and
.B XAUTHORITY
select and authorize X11 access. There is no command-line display or socket
override. Borealis reads no configuration file and does not use WME or
XDG_RUNTIME_DIR to communicate.
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
borealis window ids \-\-all \-\-group 3
borealis window count \-\-all \-\-name 'terminal|editor'
borealis window geometry 0x01234567
borealis group add 3 0x01234567
borealis group activate 3 . window focus 0x01234567
borealis window move \-\-relative 10 \-20
borealis window resize 800 600 0x01234567
borealis window layer overlay 0x01234567
borealis window apply-geometries 0x01234567 10 20 800 600
borealis wm config gap_width top 45
borealis wm config pointer_actions move resize_side resize_corner
.fi
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR aureole (1),
.BR aether (1),
.BR aetherd (1)
